"""HJ-A2: the definite Hecke algebra T (x) F_l of M(O_D; Sym^{k-2}), localised at its maximal ideals, through HACS.

What is computed (status: computed; cross-checked by an independent pure-Python route):

* The algebra is the **anemic** Hecke algebra ``T^{(D l)}``: generated by ``T_p`` for ``p`` not dividing ``D*l`` only (no
  ``T_l``, no ``U_D``/Atkin-Lehner operators).  HACS ``ReadLocal`` includes ``T_l``, so a comparison with it must account
  for this difference.  (At weight 2 adding ``T_l`` was re-checked per cell: the Z-lattice does not change, recorded as
  ``T_ell_adds_nothing``.)  Generation certificate (``generation`` per cell): at ``k = 2`` the HNF basis of the computed
  Z-span ``T_comp`` inside ``Z^{h^2}`` has all Smith invariants 1 (saturated), hence ``T_comp = Q T_comp cap M_h(Z) contains T``
  and, with ``T_comp`` inside ``T``, ``T = T_comp`` (method ``saturation``).  At ``k > 2`` with all blocks etale, ``T_comp (x) Z_l``
  is a maximal order, so ``T_l = T_comp (x) Z_l`` (method ``etale_maximal_order``).  Otherwise only the heuristic
  ``stable_under_extra_primes`` is available (method ``heuristic``).
* Class order: at ``k = 2`` the basis is the class order of ``brandt.module(D)`` as returned by ``m.T(p)``; the Brandt
  matrices themselves are exported by HJ-B0 (``code/results/s06/brandt_free_block_z/``), not here.  The local invariants do
  not depend on the order (conjugation).

* ``T`` is the **intrinsic** Z-algebra generated by the Brandt operators ``T_p`` (``p`` prime to ``D*l``) inside
  ``End_Q(M)``, ``M = M(O_D; Sym^{k-2}) (x) Q``.  It is computed as a Z-lattice (closure of the Z-span of the monomials
  in the ``T_p``, HNF) and **not** as the image of the ``T_p`` on a Z-lattice of ``M``: the image on ``L/lL`` of a
  lattice ``L`` can be a proper quotient of ``T/lT`` (this matters exactly when the answer is interesting), whereas the
  regular representation of ``T`` on itself mod ``l`` is faithful.  Consequently no integral structure of ``V_k^{Gamma_x}``
  is needed: the field ``Q(sqrt a)`` of ``symn.py`` (weight ``k > 2``) is handled by viewing ``M (x) Q(sqrt a)`` as
  a rational space of dimension ``2 dim M`` (``z = r + s sqrt a`` acts on ``(1, sqrt a)`` by ``[[r, a s], [s, r]]``;
  the Z-algebra generated is isomorphic to ``T``), and neither "``l`` inert / ramified in ``Q(sqrt a)``" nor
  "``l`` divides ``|Gamma_x|``" can affect ``T``.  Both facts are recorded per cell nevertheless (``flags``).
* ``T/lT`` acts on itself; the matrices ``C_p`` (``T_p`` in a Z-basis of ``T``, reduced mod ``l``) are emitted as
  ``matrix(l, rows)`` literals.  Over F_l the maximal ideals ``m`` of ``T/l`` correspond to the blocks of the primary
  decomposition ``T/l = prod T_m/l``; HACS has no verb that localises, so the blocks are cut out in Python
  (generalised eigenspaces ``ker f(x)^e`` of successive generators and random elements of the algebra until each block
  is local) and the **block-restricted** operators are handed to HACS: ``algebra_invariants`` (dim, ``local``,
  residue degree ``f``, dim rad, nilpotency index = Loewy length, dim tangent, embdim, dim socle, Gorenstein,
  Hilbert function), ``loewy`` and ``minpoly`` (of the generator of largest minpoly degree).  The whole algebra is also read by HACS
  (``components`` must equal the number of blocks, ``dim`` the rank of ``T``).
* Terminology.  ``m`` is **etale** iff ``T_m/l`` is reduced, i.e. a field (``dim_radical == 0``), and **strictly etale**
  iff moreover ``dim T_m/l == 1`` (residue degree 1, multiplicity free).  "Non-etale" means ``dim_radical > 0``
  (Mazur's Eisenstein ideal at the numerator of ``(D-1)/12``).  Gorenstein: ``dim soc(T_m/l) == f`` (``== 1`` when ``f = 1``).
* Weight 2: ``M`` is the full Brandt module (includes the Eisenstein line, ``dim = dim S_2^new + 1``), so Eisenstein and
  cuspidal parts are glued in ``T``.  Weight ``k > 2``: ``M`` is the ``D``-new part ``S_k(Gamma_0(D))^new`` (``symn.py``
  docstring: ``dim M = dim S_k^new + [k = 2]``), no Eisenstein line.
* Prime set: all ``p`` prime to ``D*l`` up to the Sturm-type bound ``ceil(k psi(D)/12)``, ``psi(D) = prod (p+1)``,
  and at least ``MIN_PRIMES`` of them; then primes are added two at a time until adding them does not change the
  Z-lattice ``T`` (``stable``), capped at ``PRIME_CAP``.  The count and the list are recorded.
* Size rule (plan R7): matrices as literals stay below ``10^4`` entries each (``rank T <= 100``); a cell above is refused.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import math
import pathlib
